Select the bootstrap Rust toolchain with a toolchain type, not with a build setting.

Companion patch: rules_rs-0.2.1-bootstrap-toolchain-type.patch. Apply both, or neither.

# The problem

The normal Rust toolchain depends on `@rules_rust//util/process_wrapper:process_wrapper`. The process wrapper is a
Rust binary, so it also needs a Rust toolchain. To prevent a dependency cycle, rules_rust declares a second toolchain,
the `_bootstrap` toolchain. Its `process_wrapper` is `bootstrap_process_wrapper`, a `cc_binary`.

Before this patch, the build setting `@rules_rust//rust/private:bootstrap_setting` selected the bootstrap toolchain.
`rust_binary_without_process_wrapper` had a rule transition that set the setting to `True`. Each toolchain declaration
had a `target_settings` entry, `:bootstrapping` or `:bootstrapped`.

A rule transition changes the configuration of the target and of all its dependencies. The dependencies of the process
wrapper include the cc toolchain. For the hermetic `@llvm` toolchain, the cc toolchain includes its runtime libraries,
for example compiler-rt. These targets do not read `bootstrap_setting`. But Bazel builds them again in the new
configuration, because the configuration is different.

# Example, before this patch

The command `bazel build //fleet/native/printenv/...` on macOS arm64 gives this path:

    bazel cquery 'somepath(//fleet/native/printenv:printenv_binary_x86_64-unknown-linux-musl,
                           @@llvm++llvm+llvm-project//compiler-rt:builtins)'

    @frc//:serde_derive-1.0.228 (201d1cb)                         <- a proc-macro, in the exec configuration
    @default_rust_toolchains//rustc:default_macos_aarch64_1_93_1_rust_toolchain (201d1cb)
    @rules_rust//util/process_wrapper:process_wrapper (82e7741)   <- the rule transition
    @llvm//toolchain:macos_aarch64_cc_toolchain (82e7741)
    @llvm//toolchain/args/macos:default_libs (82e7741)
    @llvm//runtimes/compiler-rt:clang_rt.builtins.static (82e7741)
    @@llvm++llvm+llvm-project//compiler-rt:builtins (c36c171)

The two exec configurations are different only in the setting:

    bazel config 201d1cb 82e7741
    FragmentOptions user-defined {
      @@rules_rs++rules_rust+rules_rust//rust/private:bootstrap_setting: null, true
    }

The llvm stage0 transition of compiler-rt keeps the setting. So Bazel compiles compiler-rt two times for the exec
platform, in two output directories:

    bazel-out/darwin_arm64-opt-exec-ST-2ca6e5d5978b/bin/external/llvm++llvm+llvm-project/compiler-rt   281 actions
    bazel-out/darwin_arm64-opt-exec-ST-2555a4bcebed/bin/external/llvm++llvm+llvm-project/compiler-rt   281 actions

Bazel also analyzes the cc toolchain, the Rust toolchain and their dependencies two times.

# Why other fixes do not work

- `--experimental_propagate_custom_flag` controls which custom flags pass through an exec transition. There is no exec
  transition between the process wrapper and the cc toolchain, so the flag has no effect.
- The llvm runtime transitions cannot reset `bootstrap_setting`. The llvm module does not depend on rules_rust, and a
  Starlark transition must declare its outputs statically.
- Path mapping (`--experimental_output_paths=strip`) can only share cache entries. Analysis still forks, and the
  second output directory stays.
- Bazel cannot apply a transition to only one toolchain type of a rule.

# The fix

This patch adds the toolchain type `@rules_rust//rust:bootstrap_toolchain_type`. The toolchain declarations, not a
build setting, now separate the two toolchains:

    toolchain(
        name = "..._bootstrap",
        toolchain = "..._rust_toolchain_bootstrap",              # bootstrapping = True
        toolchain_type = "@rules_rust//rust:bootstrap_toolchain_type",
    )

These rules ask for the new type, and they have no transition:

- `rust_binary_without_process_wrapper` (the process wrapper)
- `rust_library_without_process_wrapper` (tinyjson)
- `rust_static_library_without_process_wrapper` (the allocator library of the process wrapper)
- the new `rust_allocator_libraries_without_process_wrapper` (the allocator libraries of the process wrapper)

A rule asks for the bootstrap toolchain like this:

    rust_binary_without_process_wrapper = rule(
        ...
        toolchains = [
            str(Label("//rust:bootstrap_toolchain_type")),
            config_common.toolchain_type("@bazel_tools//tools/cpp:toolchain_type", mandatory = False),
        ],
    )

`find_toolchain(ctx)` returns the bootstrap toolchain when the rule asked for its type. Otherwise it returns the
`//rust:toolchain_type` toolchain. So the code that reads `toolchain._bootstrapping` does not change.

The cycle cannot come back. The bootstrap toolchain uses `bootstrap_process_wrapper`, a `cc_binary`, which asks for no
Rust toolchain. Each dependency of the process wrapper that asks for a Rust toolchain must ask for the bootstrap type.
That is why `empty_allocator_libraries_without_process_wrapper` and the allocator rule variant are new. A
`rust_allocator_libraries` target in that graph gives this cycle:

    process_wrapper -> empty_allocator_libraries -> //rust:toolchain_type toolchain -> process_wrapper

The process wrapper is now compatible with all configurations. Its `target_compatible_with` select on
`:bootstrapping` is removed, because the setting is always false now.

# Example, after this patch

    @frc//:serde_derive-1.0.228 (201d1cb)
    @default_rust_toolchains//rustc:default_macos_aarch64_1_93_1_rust_toolchain (201d1cb)
    @rules_rust//util/process_wrapper:process_wrapper (201d1cb)   <- no transition

The process wrapper shares the cc toolchain and compiler-rt with all other exec targets. Only
`darwin_arm64-opt-exec-ST-2ca6e5d5978b` compiles compiler-rt for the exec platform. For
`//fleet/native/printenv/...`, the compiler-rt compile actions go from 1755 to 1474.

# Compatibility

- `bootstrap_setting`, `:bootstrapped` and `:bootstrapping` stay, but no rule sets the setting now. rules_rs writes
  the setting in `rs/private/source_stdlib.bzl`, and `@rules_rs//rs/toolchains:all_targets` selects on the config
  settings. A removal would break them.
- `BUILD_for_toolchain` in `rust/private/repository_utils.bzl` registers the `_bootstrap` toolchain
  under the new type. It does this only for a Rust toolchain, not for rustfmt or rust-analyzer.
- A module that declares its own Rust toolchains must register the `_bootstrap` toolchain under
  `@rules_rust//rust:bootstrap_toolchain_type`, without the `:bootstrapping` target setting. Otherwise
  `rust_binary_without_process_wrapper` finds no toolchain. rules_rs does this in the companion patch.
- `rust_test_without_process_wrapper_test` keeps `//rust:toolchain_type`, because it uses the process wrapper.

